These are the failure cases, with the safer pattern after them.
Cases that fail with createDataFrame(records)
1. Same field has different types across rows (int vs float)
This is common in real data, for example story points on ADO work items.
records = [
    {"id": 1, "storyPoints": 3},      # int
    {"id": 2, "storyPoints": 2.5},    # float
]
spark.createDataFrame(records)
# PySparkTypeError: [CANNOT_MERGE_TYPE] Can not merge type LongType and DoubleType
2. Field is a string in some rows and an object in others
records = [
    {"id": 1, "assignee": "alice"},
    {"id": 2, "assignee": {"name": "bob", "id": 42}},
]
spark.createDataFrame(records)
# CANNOT_MERGE_TYPE: StringType and StructType
3. Field is null in every row of the batch
GitHub issues do this with milestone or closed_at when nothing in the batch has a value.
